2. THE FAILED CASE, REPRODUCED, AND WHAT THE PROTECTION NOW ADMITS
   T10 10j (c) and (e) (record l9t5, l9t5_t10.out): 'VOS0 under the trip takes the controller past its own 105 C VOS0 limit from 0.1936 A',
     '(112.7 C at 0.2452 A)'; cx46 finding 17: 'the MCU reaches its 105 C VOS0 PRINTED LIMIT at approximately 0.1936 A, MODEL'
   reproduced (MODEL on PRINTED): the junction at 76.25 C inside air (L4-E12 E5, the case's) on LQFP100's 45.0 C/W (DS12110 Rev 11
     Table 222, p.344) at 3.3 V: 105 C at 0.1936 A; 112.7 C at 0.2452 A (the removed rail trip's average maximum)
   the 105 C itself: DS12110 Rev 11 Table 112 note 5, p.210: "5. TJMax = 105 °C.", on the VOS0 rows; Table 113, p.210: VOS0 LDO, Max TJ 105 C,
     480 MHz, VDDLDO from 1.7 V; VOS1 to VOS3: 125 C. Table 111 (absolute maximum ratings), p.208: TJ 125 C
   the VOS0 rows ST prints (Table 119, p.215, revision V, LDO ON, the maxima by characterization, its note 2; mA):
     480 MHz, all peripherals disabled    typ 148 (TYPICAL)   max 226 / 307 / 390 at TJ 25 / 85 / 105 C (PRINTED)
     480 MHz, all peripherals enabled     typ 226 (TYPICAL)   max 348 / 439 / 550 at TJ 25 / 85 / 105 C (PRINTED)
   the operating point on the printed maxima (TJ = air + theta x VDD x Imax(TJ), linear between the printed columns; MODEL):
     peripherals disabled: none at or under 105 C (the least excess over the column's own TJ +29.2 K; 134.2 C at the 105 C column)
     peripherals enabled:  none at or under 105 C (the least excess +52.9 K; 157.9 C at the 105 C column)
   the protection now: W138's selection (record l4reg, SESSION W138-1, filed in inputs/) puts a TPS2553-1 ahead of each supervisor's
     regulator with IOS 0.4702 to 0.5878 A (W159's envelope of TI's tested row and its Equation 1, record l4reg at fnd/l4hod 23384006, filed
     in inputs/; W138's tested row gave 0.4702 to 0.5704 A; W167 on W163-N4) and removes round 6's rail trip (W138-2); its finding L4REG-F7 hands the controller's protection
     to this task. At 0.5878 A (all of it in the controller, the brief's convention) the junction would read 163.5 C; the controller's own
     VOS0 maximum at 105 C, 0.550 A, lies inside the limiter's band, so whether the limiter ever acts on a VOS0 state is not printed,
     and from 0.1936 A to the band's bottom it never does
   so HO-E is not a current limit's problem: no printed VOS0 row has an operating point inside 105 C at this air, at any current a
   regulator stage passes. W127's finding 2 stands (0.1855 A served against 0.1936 A, a 4.4 % band) and is not the whole of it: VOS0
   must be kept out, or ended before the junction moves. The approaches below are judged on that.

3. H-1, A HARDWARE-FORCED VOLTAGE SCALE: what the maker's documents print (each sentence matched in its pinned text)
   RM0433 Rev 8 p.264, 6.4.2:                     "The regulator output voltage can be scaled by software to different voltage levels (VOS0(a), VOS1, VOS2, and VOS3) that are configured through VOS bits in PWR D3 domain control register (PWR_D3CR)."
   RM0433 Rev 8 p.264:                            "By default VOS3 is selected after system reset. VOS can be changed on-the-fly to adapt to the required system performance."
   RM0433 Rev 8 p.279, 6.6.2:                     "After reset, the system starts on the lowest Run mode voltage scaling (VOS3). The voltage scaling can then be changed on-the-fly by software by programming VOS bits in PWR D3 domain control register (PWR_D3CR)"
   RM0433 Rev 8 p.279:                            "The system maximum frequency can be reached by boosting the voltage scaling level to VOS0. This is done through the ODEN bit in the SYSCFG_PWRCR register."
   RM0433 Rev 8 p.280, Note:                      "VOS0 can be enabled only when VOS1 is programmed in PWR D3 domain control register (PWR_D3CR) VOS bits."
   RM0433 Rev 8 p.309, 6.8.6 PWR_D3CR:            "Bits 15:14 VOS: Voltage scaling selection according to performance"
   RM0433 Rev 8 p.309:                            "01: Scale 3 (default)"
   RM0433 Rev 8 p.560, 12.3.10 SYSCFG_PWRCR:      "Bit 0 ODEN: Overdrive enable, this bit allows to activate the LDO regulator overdrive mode."
   RM0433 Rev 8 p.560:                            "This bit must be written only in VOS1 voltage scaling mode."
   RM0433 Rev 8 p.560:                            "1: Overdrive mode enabled (the LDO generates VOS0 for VCORE)"
   RM0433 Rev 8 p.260, Table 34 (LDO Bypass):     "VCORE supplied from external source"
   RM0433 Rev 8 p.262:                            "Due to the LDO default state after power-up (enabled by default), the external VCORE voltage must remain higher than 1.1 V until the LDO is disabled by software."
   RM0433 Rev 8 p.306, 6.8.4 PWR_CR3:             "This register is reset only by POR. It is not reset by wakeup from Standby mode and by the RESET pad."
   RM0433 Rev 8 p.307, 6.8.4 PWR_CR3:             "The lower byte of this register is written once after POR and shall be written before changing VOS level or ck_sys clock frequency."
   DS12110 Rev 11 p.29, 3.5.3:                    "Scale 0: boosted performance (available only with LDO regulator)"
   DS12110 Rev 11 p.210, Table 112 note 4:        "4. VOS0 is available only when the LDO regulator is ON."
   AN4938 Rev 7 p.10, 2.1.4:                      "The LDO voltage regulator is always enabled after reset with a default output level set to power scale 3 (VOS3)."
   AN4938 Rev 7 p.19, 2.3.7:                      "The power management unit can be bypassed. This feature can be configured by software."
   AN4938 Rev 7 p.19:                             "In Bypass mode, the internal voltage scaling is not managed internally, and the external voltage value must be consistent with the targeted maximum frequency"
   the option bytes (RM0433 Rev 8 p.215 to 216, 4.9.9 FLASH_OPTSR_PRG, the user option word a part keeps across resets): 11 fields,
     SWAP_BANK_OPT, IO_HSLV, SECURITY, ST_RAM_SIZE[1:0], IWDG_FZ_SDBY, IWDG_FZ_STOP, RDP, NRST_STDY_D1, NRST_STOP_D1, IWDG1_SW, BOR_LEV; a field that selects a voltage scale or the supply configuration: NONE
   the PWR pins (RM0433 Rev 8 p.256, Table 32, 'PWR input/output signals connected to package pins or balls'): VDD Main I/O and VDD domain supply input; VDDA External analog power supply for analog peripherals; VREF+,VREF- External reference voltage for ADCs and DAC; VBAT Backup battery supply input; VDDLDO Voltage regulator supply input; VCAP Digital core domain supply; VDD50USB USB regulator supply input; VDD33USB USB regulator supply output; VSS Main ground; AHB AHB register interface; PDR_ON Power Down Reset enable
   READING: the voltage scale is a software choice made after every reset (PWR_D3CR's VOS bits, then ODEN for VOS0); no pin, strap or
   option byte selects or caps it. The one hardware means the documents print is the Bypass supply (an external regulator on VCAP; VOS0
   'available only with LDO regulator'), and that configuration is itself written by software once after every POR, with the LDO enabled
   by default until then; in Bypass the clock is still the firmware's ('must be consistent with the targeted maximum frequency').
   PWR_CR3's LOCK (W144's F9, RM0433 p.306 and p.307 above): the supply configuration is reset only by POR, not by the RESET pad, and is
   written once after POR; after that first write no later firmware fault can change it before the next POR, so a Bypass
   configuration written first would keep the LDO, and with it VOS0, off until then. It is still not a bar: the first write is made
   by whatever image runs first, and an image that writes the LDO configuration (or none) keeps the LDO on.
   VERDICT H-1: DROPS OUT (the register's end condition: the documents show no hardware means). Not claimed: any hardware bar on VOS0.
   The Bypass route would only move the firmware dependency to the first write after POR, at the cost of three external core
   regulators, and ST's run-current rows are printed with the 'LDO regulator ON' (Table 119's title): not pursued.

4. H-3, A THERMAL-HEADROOM PART OR HEAT PATH: the junction-to-ambient VOS0 would need at 76.25 C air (MODEL on PRINTED)
   VOS0 480 MHz, all peripherals enabled, 0.550 A at 105 C:  at most 15.84 C/W
   VOS0 480 MHz, all peripherals disabled, 0.390 A at 105 C: at most 22.34 C/W
   the protection's largest admitted current, 0.5878 A (the brief's convention, all in the controller): at most 14.82 C/W
   printed (DS12110 Rev 11 Table 222, p.344 to 345; JESD51-2 still air, its reference 8.10.1):
     LQFP100        ThetaJA  45.0   ThetaJB  36.3   ThetaJC  11.5 C/W
     TFBGA100       ThetaJA  39.3   ThetaJB  21.1   ThetaJC  17.1 C/W
     LQFP144        ThetaJA  43.7   ThetaJB  38.3   ThetaJC  11.3 C/W
     UFBGA169       ThetaJA  37.7   ThetaJB  17.3   ThetaJC  11.0 C/W
     LQFP176        ThetaJA  43.0   ThetaJB  39.4   ThetaJC  11.2 C/W
     LQFP208        ThetaJA  42.4   ThetaJB  40.3   ThetaJC  11.1 C/W
     UFBGA176+25    ThetaJA  37.4   ThetaJB  19.3   ThetaJC  23.9 C/W
     TFBGA240+25    ThetaJA  36.6   ThetaJB  24.3   ThetaJC   7.4 C/W
   at one supply corner (W145-4, W144's F8): the same row at the rail's top 3.3577 V needs at most 15.57 C/W (the figure carried below)
   the least ThetaJA of any package is TFBGA240+25's 36.6 C/W, 2.4x the need; the fitted LQFP100 reads 45.0 C/W
   every ThetaJB printed (the least 17.3 C/W, UFBGA169) is over the need: no path through the board reaches it on printed figures
   a heat path through the case top (W144's F10): the LQFP100's ThetaJC 11.5 C/W leaves 4.07 C/W, the TFBGA240+25's 7.4 C/W leaves 8.17 C/W
     (a package change on board B's supervisors; W144's 4.34 and 8.44 C/W are the same on the 3.3 V figure), for the interface, a heat
     sink and its path to the 76.25 C air inside the sealed case;
     a path from the top to the case wall (cooler than the inside air) is a third form; no held document prints a heat sink, an
     interface material, a strap or a wall path for these parts (the one cooler held is the CM5's), and none prints board B's pockets
   the air at which VOS0's enabled maximum holds 105 C on the printed 45.0 C/W: 23.3 C, against the case's 76.25 C
   the regulator's own theta (W138's TPS73733DCQRM3, 76.0 C/W PRINTED, record l4reg) moves the REGULATOR's junction; the controller's
     105 C current, (105 - air) / (theta x VDD) = 0.1936 A, does not depend on it: no regulator part acts on HO-E
   VERDICT H-3: NOT ESTABLISHED ON PRINTED FIGURES (no package's ThetaJA and no printed path reaches 15.57 C/W at this air; a top or
   wall path would rest on a heat sink, an interface and a mechanical path that no held document prints: not impossible, not
   established, not selected).

5. H-2, A FIRMWARE BOUND WITH AN INDEPENDENT ENDING
   5a. THE FIRMWARE BOUND (FW-B20, DRAFTED in record l9t5, unapplied): 'Run each supervisor at VOS3 with HCLK at most 144 MHz (no PLL above it)'. It is a property of an image that does not exist
       yet; it is verified, not enforced: the image's writes to PWR_D3CR (p.309) and SYSCFG_PWRCR (p.560) read by a static check. Alone
       it ends nothing: a corrupted or wrong image can still select VOS0 (section 3)
   5b. THE IWDG (RM0433 Rev 8 p.1896, 45.3.4): "If the “Hardware watchdog” feature is enabled through the device option bits, the watchdog is automatically enabled at power-on, and generates a reset unless the IWDG key register (IWDG_KR) is written by the software before the counter reaches end of count or if the downcounter is reloaded inside the window."
       a running firmware that selects VOS0 and keeps writing IWDG_KR is never reset by it: NOT AN ENDING for HO-E (it ends hangs)
   5c. THE PEERS (W137's M-B vote, fnd/l4canmb 0d079eaf, T10-ROUND6.md filed in inputs/): each peer observes the other controllers' TXD
       lines and votes their transceivers' SHDN (W137-D3: 'the vote acts on SHDN, not on the LDO's EN'). A controller in VOS0 can still
       send good frames, and a silenced transceiver leaves its controller running: NOT AN ENDING for HO-E. Their use here is detection
       of a missed self-test (5e), through the state frame they already read
   5d. THE VCORE MONITOR, DRAFTED (apply_gen_sch_b_vcoremon.py): a TI TPS37 per supervisor, channel 1 overvoltage (option 01, 800 mV,
       an external divider), open-drain active-low RESET1 into a hold stage that drives that controller's NRST through 390R 1% (W148-1), VDD
       on the controller's own 3.3 V.
       What it reads: VCAP, the core voltage ST prints per scale (DS12110 Rev 11 Table 112, p.209, LDO ON, min/typ/max):
         VOS3 0.95 / 1.00 / 1.05 V
         VOS2 1.05 / 1.10 / 1.15 V
         VOS1 1.15 / 1.20 / 1.26 V
         VOS0 1.26 / 1.35 / 1.40 V
       TPS37 rows (SNVSBJ1E): VITP 0.792 / 0.800 / 0.808 V at VIT 800 mV (7.5, p.7); ISENSE at most 100 nA (p.7); hysteresis options VHYS Range = 2% to 13%,
       VHYS between Hyst% x 0.985 and x 1.015 of VIT+(OV)MIN (Figure 7-1, p.10); the 2 % option is the draft's
       the divider: the E96 top over 100k 0.1% 25ppm that keeps the trip band inside (VOS3's top 1.05 V, VOS1's bottom 1.15 V) and the release
       over VOS3's top, with the largest least margin (SESSION W140-2; resistors at 0.1 % plus 25 ppm/K over 65 K, ISENSE either way):
         top 34.8k 0.1% 25ppm  trip 1.0627 to 1.0942 V, release from 1.0410 V, least margin -0.0090 V
         top 35.7k 0.1% 25ppm  trip 1.0697 to 1.1016 V, release from 1.0479 V, least margin -0.0021 V
         top 36.5k 0.1% 25ppm  trip 1.0759 to 1.1081 V, release from 1.0540 V, least margin +0.0040 V
         top 37.4k 0.1% 25ppm  trip 1.0829 to 1.1155 V, release from 1.0608 V, least margin +0.0108 V
         top 38.3k 0.1% 25ppm  trip 1.0899 to 1.1229 V, release from 1.0677 V, least margin +0.0177 V
         top 39.2k 0.1% 25ppm  trip 1.0969 to 1.1303 V, release from 1.0746 V, least margin +0.0197 V   SELECTED
         top 40.2k 0.1% 25ppm  trip 1.1047 to 1.1386 V, release from 1.0822 V, least margin +0.0114 V
         top 41.2k 0.1% 25ppm  trip 1.1125 to 1.1468 V, release from 1.0898 V, least margin +0.0032 V
         top 42.2k 0.1% 25ppm  trip 1.1202 to 1.1550 V, release from 1.0974 V, least margin -0.0050 V
         top 43.2k 0.1% 25ppm  trip 1.1280 to 1.1632 V, release from 1.1050 V, least margin -0.0132 V
       so every VCAP in VOS3's printed band leaves the monitor released (1.0969 V over 1.05 V), and every VCAP in VOS1's or VOS0's band
       trips it (1.1303 V under 1.15 V): any VOS1 entry, and so any VOS0 entry (RM0433 p.280's note), resets the controller to VOS3
       (p.279). VOS2's band (1.05 to 1.15 V) straddles the trip: a VOS2 entry is caught only above 1.1303 V (section 6, S-f)
       the reset state the ending relies on: "When a system reset occurs, the voltage regulator is enabled and supplies VCORE." (RM0433 p.263); PWR_D3CR "Reset value: 0x0000 4000 (Following reset VOSRDY will be read 1 by software)." (p.309: VOS = 01, Scale 3);
       SYSCFG_PWRCR "Reset Value: 0x0000 0000" (p.560: ODEN = 0). Which resets restore them is PRINTED (W144's F5): "A system reset (nreset) resets all registers to their reset values unless otherwise specified in the register description." with "A reset from NRST pin (external reset)" among
       its sources (8.4.2, p.329), and "In case of an external reset, the reset pulse is generated while the NRST pin is asserted Low."; Table 55 (p.330), the NRST row: "Resets VDD domain: IWDG1, LDO...", "Debug features, Flash memory, RTC and backup RAM are not reset". PWR_D3CR's and
       SYSCFG_PWRCR's descriptions name no exception, where PWR_CR3's does (p.306: "reset only by POR"): an NRST reset restores
       VOS = Scale 3 and ODEN = 0 by the manual's own words; S2's ACTVOS reading confirms a printed fact on the specimen
       the ending's timing, VCAP over the trip to the controller in reset (the chain of SESSION W148-1: the TPS37's RESET1 drives the
       hold stage's manual reset, the hold stage's RESET drives NRST; section 5h compares it):
         TPS37 sense delay tCTS 8 us typ, 17 us max at VIT 800 mV with CTS open, '20% Overdrive from VIT' (7.6, p.9, PRINTED);
           VOS0's bottom 1.26 V is 11.5 % over the trip's top, VOS1's 1.15 V is 1.7 %: under 20 %, so the printed maximum does NOT cover
           these entries (supplier task S2); 17 us is carried below as the figure S2 must confirm, never as a bound
         RESET1 into the TPS3703's MR (TI SBVS249B): RESET1 at most 300 mV while it sinks 5 mA or less (TPS37 p.7) against VMR_L at most
