   5e. ITS OWN FAULTS AND THEIR SELF-TEST (SESSION W140-3 as restated by W145-2, W145-3 and W148-3; the firmware row is FW-B23,
       drafted in apply_hw_fw_contract_hoe.py for L4A-61; nothing applied): at every start and then once every 3600 s, at HCLK
       at most 144 MHz, the controller clears the reset flags ("The CPU can reset the flags by setting RMVF bit.", RM0433 8.4.4, p.332), keeps a marker and the RTC's
       time where NRST does not reach (Table 55, p.330: "Debug features, Flash memory, RTC and backup RAM are not reset"), writes VOS = Scale 1 and waits at most 122.6 us from that
       write (t_resp: the window timed from the write covers the regulator's ramp, the monitor, the hold stage and NRST's fall on every
       unit in service, W144's F11); still running, it writes Scale 3 within 10 us (W145-2) and reports MONITOR FAILED in its
       state frame. After the reset, PASSED needs the marker and RCC_RSR equal to Table 56's row 2, 'Pin reset (NRST)' (RM0433 8.4.4,
       p.332): PINRSTF and CPURSTF set, LPWRRSTF, WWDG1RSTF, IWDG1RSTF, SFTRSTF, PORRSTF, BORRSTF, D2RSTF, D1RSTF clear (W145-3),
       and (W148-3, restated by SESSION W152-1 on W150's F4) the RTC's time from the write to the restart at least 12 ms, else HOLD
       FAILED. The time base: the RTC clocked by the LSI, which a pin reset does not stop (PRINTED by reference, V-B24 confirms it):
       RCC_BDCR, "RCC_BDCR bits are only reset after a backup domain reset (see Section 8.4.6: Backup domain reset). Any other internal or external reset will not have any effect on these bits." (RM0433 8.7.26,
       p.426), RTCSEL "10: LSI clock used as RTC clock"; LSIRDY "This bit can be set even when LSION is not enabled if there is a request for LSI clock by the Clock Security System on LSE or by the Low Speed Watchdog or by the RTC." (8.7.27, p.428); never the HSE: "If HSE is selected as RTC clock: this clock is lost when the system is in Stop mode or in case of a pin reset (NRST)." (p.426);
       board B draws no LSE crystal (each controller's PC14 and PC15, pins 8 and 9, open: section 8 reads them). The LSI runs at 29.4 to
       33.6 kHz over TJ -40 to 125 C, 32 kHz typical (DS12110 Rev 11 Table 136, p.233, PRINTED); the sub-second counter runs on
       ck_apre: "The ck_apre clock is used to clock the binary RTC_SSR subseconds downcounter." (46.3.4, p.1910), "ck_apre frequency = RTCCLK frequency/(PREDIV_A+1)" (46.6.5, p.1932; RTC_PRER: "System reset: not affected"), with PREDIV_A + 1
       at most 8: a step of at most 0.250 ms read at the typical 32 kHz (W150: the 1 ms resolution and the +-40 % clock were too coarse)
         a healthy hold (at least 14 ms, PRINTED) reads at least 14 ms x 29.4 / 32 kHz - 0.250 ms = 12.613 ms: over the 12 ms threshold
         a restart reads under 12 ms while its true interval is under (12 - 0.250) ms x 32 / 33.6 kHz = 11.190 ms; that interval is
         t_resp 122.6 us, the TPS37's release tCTR(no cap) at most 40 us (p.9), the hold, NRST's rise to VIH 0.7 x VDD (Table 147, p.241)
         at most 1.254 ms (MODEL: from 0 V through 10 k at +5 % (ASSUMPTION) and RPU 50 kOhm into 100 nF at +20 %), VCAP's fall to the
         release and the boot to the RTC's read (with its shadow registers' resynchronisation): a lost CT pull-up (the hold at most
         1.3 ms, 7.6) reads HOLD FAILED while those last two stay under 8.474 ms (ASSUMPTION; V-B24 reads them on the first article),
         and any hold degraded under 9.774 ms less them reads HOLD FAILED too (W150's F4: a component fault outside printed limits)
         W148-3's 7 ms on an RTC within +-40 % at 1 ms resolution left 3.577 ms ('3.6 ms') with tCTR, VCAP's fall and the resolution left
         out: withdrawn by W152-1. A start that follows a test (the marker set) evaluates it and does not test again (W150's F6)
       PINRSTF alone is not enough: "Bit 22 PINRSTF: Pin reset flag (NRST) (1)", "Set by hardware when a reset from pin occurs." (8.7.39, p.450), and Table 56 sets it in the rows Power-on reset (pwr_por_rst); Brownout reset (pwr_bor_rst); System reset generated by CPU (SFTRESET); WWDG1 reset (wwdg1_out_rst); IWDG1 reset (iwdg1_out_rst); D1 erroneously enters DStandby mode or CPU erroneously enters CStop mode too
       (W144's F3). Scale 1 (VCAP 1.15 V and up) is over the trip's top by 1.7 %: the test drives the whole real path, VCAP to divider to
       SENSE1 to RESET1 to MR to RESET to NRST. One supervisor tests at a time and only while the other two serve (IOHA row 3); the peers
       flag a supervisor whose test counter has not moved for 2 x 3600 s (FW-B22's state frame). A healthy unit whose ramp makes the
       interval from the write longer than t_resp reads MONITOR FAILED: found at the first article by S2, and then the window and S1's
       limit are re-read together (W145-2's reversal)
         divider top open or bottom shorted                     SENSE1 at 0 V: never trips                                 found: the next test (no reset)
         divider bottom open or top shorted                     SENSE1 at VCAP (1.0 V and up) over 0.808 V: held in reset  found: at once (the controller silent, IOHA row 3)
         a divider value drifted                                the band moves                                             found: the test if the trip passes VOS1's bottom; else at once (held in reset)
         RESET1 stuck released, or MONRST open at MR            never resets                                               found: the next test
         RESET1 stuck low, or MONRST shorted to ground          held in reset                                              found: at once
         the TPS37 unpowered (VDD open)                         output undefined under VPOR                                found: the next test, or at once if it rests low
         SENSE1 and SENSE2 exchanged or shorted (assembly)      SENSE1 at the rail: held in reset                          found: at once
         a slowed monitor or hold stage (sense, MR, NRST fall)  the ending takes longer than t_resp                        found: the next test (its window is t_resp, F11)
         the hold stage's RESET stuck released, or R812 open    never resets                                               found: the next test
         the hold stage's RESET stuck low, or MONOUT at ground  held in reset                                              found: at once
         the hold stage unpowered (VDD open)                    RESET undefined under its VPOR                             found: the next test, or at once if it rests low
         the CT pull-up R813 open (CT floating)                 the hold falls to CT open's 0.7 to 1.3 ms                  found: the next test's hold timing (W148-3, W152-1)
         CT shorted to ground                                   TI's latch mode (9.1.3): held after a trip                 found: at once (the start's own test latches it)
         a firmware that skips the test                         a latent monitor fault stays latent                        found: the peers, within 2 x 3600 s
       the residuals: a monitor or hold fault latent since the last test, then a firmware VOS0 entry or an image that enters VOS1 or
       VOS0 at every boot: a double fault, its window at most 3600 s (S-i, S-l)
